Month 5 — Day 8: Dockerfile, Custom Images & AWS/Docker Revision

Overview

Today I worked on building custom Docker images, understanding Dockerfile instructions, image layers, caching, ".dockerignore", and publishing a custom image to Docker Hub.

I also started revising AWS and Docker concepts through practical troubleshooting and interview-style scenarios.

1. Dockerfile

Created a Dockerfile to build a custom Nginx website image.

FROM nginx:latest
COPY . /usr/share/nginx/html/

Instructions learned

- "FROM" — Defines the base image.
- "COPY" — Copies files from the build context into the image.
- "RUN" — Executes commands during image building.
- "CMD" — Defines the default command when a container starts.

2. Custom Docker Image Build

Built a custom image using:

sudo docker build -t aman-nginx:v1 .

Ran the container and verified the website in the browser.

3. Docker Image Layers & Cache

Explored image history using:

sudo docker history aman-nginx:v1

Learned how Docker images consist of layers and how Docker build cache can reuse unchanged layers to make builds more efficient.

4. .dockerignore

Created and tested a ".dockerignore" file.

.git
.gitignore
README.md
*.log

Created a temporary log file and verified that it was excluded from the Docker build context and was not present inside the resulting container.

Why .dockerignore matters

- Excludes unnecessary files from the build context.
- Helps reduce build context size.
- Prevents unwanted files from being copied into the image.

5. Docker Hub

Tagged and published my custom image to Docker Hub.

sudo docker tag aman-nginx:v3 aman1030/my-nginx:v2
sudo docker login -u aman1030
sudo docker push aman1030/my-nginx:v2

Docker Hub repository:

https://hub.docker.com/repository/docker/aman1030/my-nginx

6. AWS Revision — Troubleshooting Scenarios

Revised the following AWS concepts through practical scenarios:

- EC2 website connectivity troubleshooting.
- Security Groups and inbound/outbound rules.
- Nginx service troubleshooting.
- VPC, public/private subnets, and Route Tables.
- Internet Gateway vs NAT Gateway.
- IAM Roles and EC2 temporary credentials.
- ARN and AWS identity verification using "aws sts get-caller-identity".
- S3 IAM permissions and Bucket Policies.
- Explicit Deny.
- Security Groups vs Network ACLs.
- Stateful vs Stateless networking.
- VPC local routes and communication between EC2 instances using private IPs.

7. Key Learnings

- A Dockerfile defines how a custom Docker image is built.
- Docker images contain layers that can be reused through caching.
- ".dockerignore" filters unnecessary files from the build context.
- Docker images can be tagged and published to Docker Hub.
- IAM Roles provide temporary AWS credentials to EC2.
- Security Groups are stateful, while NACLs are stateless.
- Private EC2 instances can access the internet through a NAT Gateway without requiring public IPs.
- EC2 instances within the same VPC can communicate through private IPs using the local route, subject to security and network rules.

